{:edb/db-before-t 2838 :edb/db-after-t 2839 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536151 50 #inst "2026-10-09T17:11:09.194Z" 13194139536151 true] [17592186198328 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386238 13194139536151 true] [17592186198329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386245 13194139536151 true] [17592186198329 1020 #edb/ref 17592186386252 13194139536151 true] [17592186198545 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536151 true] [17592186198545 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/fd18335a2034613658c65186c4fc6e3e74067e730183d3e66fbacf8ab3601928.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139536151 true] [17592186198545 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4144/Source/Images/q-161830.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = ⟨0, z,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139536151 false] [17592186198545 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536151 true] [17592186198546 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536151 true] [17592186198546 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139536151 true] [17592186198546 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4144/Source/Images/q-161862.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = ⟨y, 0, 2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139536151 false] [17592186198546 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨y,0,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386238 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386239 13194139536151 true] [17592186386238 1089 "q-161899" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386238 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536151 true] [17592186386238 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/8a48be0b4c101babf157a05959e923576ad5614113b3eec9e93fe747d5dc3803.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,x,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386238 1113 #uuid "62cc77f5-d29c-5b66-aeee-e4c2bff38586" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386238 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=x,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,x,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386240 13194139536151 true] [17592186386239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386241 13194139536151 true] [17592186386239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386242 13194139536151 true] [17592186386239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386243 13194139536151 true] [17592186386239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386244 13194139536151 true] [17592186386239 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536151 true] [17592186386239 1041 "selection" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386239 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386241 13194139536151 true] [17592186386239 1110 #uuid "279c2708-6652-5ba9-a0ae-e0281f94d404" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386240 1034 "\\frac{xy-y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386240 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386240 1111 #uuid "4a069c36-b5b0-52d6-809f-19d65bb38ead" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386241 1034 "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386241 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386241 1111 #uuid "76433b57-2b2c-51bd-8789-cbff918f080c" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386242 1034 "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386242 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386242 1111 #uuid "d7690d3c-75eb-5fbd-92ed-d1158ea79456" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386243 1034 "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386243 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386243 1111 #uuid "7203265a-4390-527f-b95e-1643e43c34b5" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386244 1034 "\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386244 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386244 1111 #uuid "4a4ad1e8-fb8d-5045-adb0-4a637a9753ec" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386245 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386246 13194139536151 true] [17592186386245 1089 "q-161903" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386245 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536151 true] [17592186386245 1100 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-z,\\,2y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386245 1113 #uuid "c8fd3ad8-a86b-542f-ab4a-6226468a4f6c" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386245 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-z,2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386247 13194139536151 true] [17592186386246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386248 13194139536151 true] [17592186386246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386249 13194139536151 true] [17592186386246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386250 13194139536151 true] [17592186386246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386251 13194139536151 true] [17592186386246 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536151 true] [17592186386246 1041 "selection" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386246 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386249 13194139536151 true] [17592186386246 1110 #uuid "8924d504-3edb-5802-9332-5109510777e2" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386247 1034 "-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386247 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386247 1111 #uuid "ed3b0302-28ab-5be2-86c7-e2840bfa6cf0" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386248 1034 "2y+\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386248 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386248 1111 #uuid "b4eec892-0639-5e9d-ae67-55af7c915d07" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386249 1034 "y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386249 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386249 1111 #uuid "1514334d-b962-51ff-bc04-fd2348c7a74a" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386250 1034 "4y+\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386250 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386250 1111 #uuid "8798600b-a935-5e72-af8a-7ef618e4933c" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386251 1034 "y-\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386251 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386251 1111 #uuid "5ce8bf71-8f80-5f4d-97e5-01363250f04d" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386252 1017 #edb/ref 17592186386253 13194139536151 true] [17592186386252 1089 "q-161915" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386252 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536151 true] [17592186386252 1100 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloids $z=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386252 1113 #uuid "1c5fb359-1792-5bae-84b5-842bbac13d34" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386252 1145 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (-2x)-(0)\\cdot (-2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2xy+4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-5\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386253 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386254 13194139536151 true] [17592186386253 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386255 13194139536151 true] [17592186386253 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386256 13194139536151 true] [17592186386253 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386257 13194139536151 true] [17592186386253 1000 #edb/ref 17592186386258 13194139536151 true] [17592186386253 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536151 true] [17592186386253 1041 "selection" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386253 1104 #edb/ref 17592186386256 13194139536151 true] [17592186386253 1110 #uuid "df3fc501-8aec-587f-8354-4407ad8c7b26" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386254 1034 "2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386254 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386254 1111 #uuid "d9a1b88a-014d-541f-ba9a-48c84dbaf1ea" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386255 1034 "4xy-1" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386255 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386255 1111 #uuid "d2d8e0a8-8a62-5921-a1dc-3804fb6ca864" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386256 1034 "4xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-5" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386256 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386256 1111 #uuid "0ba42783-544e-5b07-94f7-1a6b12ab1787" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386257 1034 "xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386257 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386257 1111 #uuid "2fdc2d1f-f3e4-5c82-81f2-99ded537b932" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386258 1034 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3" 13194139536151 true] [17592186386258 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536151 true] [17592186386258 1111 #uuid "14d84f94-039f-5b5f-9505-53de3f5d6e12" 13194139536151 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-161899" 17592186386238 "q-161899/selection" 17592186386239 "q-161899/selection/66d7fb2d8c08e2f0127eb73fde4ee86124a709d5a6e8874afed43591ba8